# Java Prime Number Generator (1 to n)

Here's a Java application that systematically generates and displays all prime numbers between 1 and a specified integer n (in this case, n=20):

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumberGenerator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 20;
        System.out.println("Prime numbers between 1 and " + n + ":");

        List<Integer> primes = findPrimesUpTo(n);

        for (int i = 0; i < primes.size(); i++) {
            System.out.print(primes.get(i));
            if (i < primes.size() - 1) {
                System.out.print(", ");
            }
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static List<Integer> findPrimesUpTo(int limit) {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int number = 2; number <= limit; number++) {
            if (isPrime(number)) {
                primes.add(number);
            }
        }

        return primes;
    }

    private static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        if (num == 2) {
            return true;
        }
        if (num % 2 == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Check divisors up to square root of num
        int sqrtNum = (int) Math.sqrt(num);
        for (int i = 3; i <= sqrtNum; i += 2) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

## Output for n=20:
```
Prime numbers between 1 and 20:
2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19
```

## How This Works:

1. The `main` method sets the upper limit (n=20) and calls the prime-finding logic.
2. `findPrimesUpTo()` collects all prime numbers up to the specified limit.
3. `isPrime()` is a helper method that checks if a single number is prime using these optimizations:
   - Numbers ≤ 1 are not prime
   - 2 is the only even prime number
   - Only checks odd divisors up to the square root of the number
4. The program then prints all prime numbers in the specified range.

## Alternative Implementation (Sieve of Eratosthenes):

For better performance with larger values of n, you might want to use the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm:

```java
public static List<Integer> findPrimesUpToSieve(int limit) {
    boolean[] isComposite = new boolean[limit + 1];

    for (int i = 2; i * i <= limit; i++) {
        if (!isComposite[i]) {
            for (int j = i * i; j <= limit; j += i) {
                isComposite[j] = true;
            }
        }
    }

    List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 2; i <= limit; i++) {
        if (!isComposite[i]) {
            primes.add(i);
        }
    }

    return primes;
}
```

This alternative is more efficient for larger ranges as it eliminates multiples of each prime in sequence.

# Fetching MySQL Query Result to a Java Integer Variable

To fetch the count result from your MySQL query into a Java integer variable, you'll need to use JDBC. Here's how to do it:

## Basic Implementation

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class EmployeeCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/your_database";
        String username = "your_username";
        String password = "your_password";

        int count = 0;

        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(url, username, password);
             PreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement(
                 "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employeedetails")) {

            ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery();

            if (resultSet.next()) {
                count = resultSet.getInt(1);
            }

        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println("Employee count: " + count);
    }
}
```

## More Robust Version with Error Handling

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class EmployeeCounter {
    public static int getEmployeeCount(String dbUrl, String dbUser, String dbPass) {
        int count = 0;

        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(dbUrl, dbUser, dbPass);
             Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
             ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employeedetails")) {

            if (resultSet.next()) {
                count = resultSet.getInt(1);
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.err.println("Database error: " + e.getMessage());
            // You might want to throw an exception or handle it differently
            // e.printStackTrace();
        }

        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/your_database";
        String username = "your_username";
        String password = "your_password";

        int employeeCount = getEmployeeCount(url, username, password);
        System.out.println("Total employees: " + employeeCount);
    }
}
```

## Using PreparedStatement (if you need parameters)

If your query might have parameters later:

```java
public static int getEmployeeCount(String dbUrl, String dbUser, String dbPass, String tableName) {
    int count = 0;

    try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(dbUrl, dbUser, dbPass);
         PreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ?")) {

        statement.setString(1, tableName);
        ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery();

        if (resultSet.next()) {
            count = resultSet.getInt(1);
        }
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        System.err.println("Database error: " + e.getMessage());
    }

    return count;
}
```

